## Why this matters

Residential exposure alone assumes that people remain near their home location. In reality, everyday mobility may increase or decrease the environmental conditions people encounter.

This proof of concept provides a framework for extending exposure assessment from static residential locations toward activity spaces and travel environments.

## Next phase

The framework will next be extended using temporally resolved mobility data to investigate behavioural responses during high-exposure periods such as:

- wildfire smoke events;
- elevated PM₂.₅ episodes;
- extreme heat;
- compound heat and air-quality events.

The longer-term objective is to examine whether the ability to reduce, reschedule, or redirect mobility during hazardous environmental conditions differs across neighbourhoods and population groups.

